    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d86">

                <pb facs="0160" n="994" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg994"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 658.119/596</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Page</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-09-03T18:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">September 3, 1918, 6 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 7.15 p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1588. War Trade Board [from Sheldon]:</p>
                <p>No. 1359. Department’s 1008, August 27, 7 p.m., your 1094. I consider that joint
                    certification of manifests of Scandinavian and Dutch vessels by British and
                    American consuls at South American ports of departure will be best solution.
                    Practice would be for A[llied] B[lockade] C[ommittee] to inform British and
                    American consuls of Allied Blockade Committee’s approval of cargo by telegram
                    from London. On receipt of this approval British consul and American consul
                    would jointly certify manifest.</p>
                <p>Plan of territorial division not considered to be so practicable. Necessity for
                    American certification of manifest is self-apparent in order that neutral
                    vessels in question may be cleared from American ports of call with greatest
                    expedition. American consular certification should be recognized by customs
                    officials at American ports as authority to permit clearance without delay of
                    additional reference of manifest to Washington. Upon receipt of your concurrence
                    Foreign Office will telegraph British consuls confirming procedure and
                    instructing them as to such joint action with American colleagues. At same time
                    American consuls should be instructed by State Department.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Page</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
